WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.940
Hi, everyone.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.790
I'm going to talk to you
today about a book that I'm

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.890
publishing in January of 2018.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:15.690
It's called 12 Rules for
Life: An Antidote to Chaos

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.310
and it's going to be published
by Penguin Random House.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.640
I'm going to tell you how it
came about first and then I'll

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.309
describe the book's
contents and then I'm going

00:00:25.309 --> 00:00:26.600
to read you a little bit of it.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:27.500
OK.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:34.130
In 2012, I did a Canadian
Broadcasting Corporation

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.700
program called Say
No to Happiness.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.200
And in that program,
I critiqued the idea

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.610
that happiness was the proper
goal of life, claiming instead

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:50.390
that it's much more useful
and effective perhaps

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.350
even for producing happiness
to look for meaning

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.120
and responsibility in life.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.120
Anyways, I received
the following email

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.680
as a consequence.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.090
Dear Dr. Petersen, your
comments on the CBC program Say

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.460
No to Happiness were both
thought-provoking and

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.720
challenging.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.700
They had me asking
bigger questions

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.340
and that is always
tremendously appealing.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.380
To be perfectly
honest, some aspects

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.480
were harder for me to
understand than others,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.970
but I was left
wanting to know more.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.610
Have you considered writing
a book for a general,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.040
rather than academic, reader?

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.090
I'm a literary agent and
co-owner at the Cook Agency.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.560
We have experience
with academics

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.870
interested in writing
for a broad audience,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.480
working with them to remove the
scaffolding that is required

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.820
in academic writing
and helping to make

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.600
their work more accessible.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.740
We specialize in selling
authors to international markets

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.990
and developing their
writing careers.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:51.360
You can find out more about
us at www.cookagency.ca.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.760
I look forward to
hearing from you.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.860
Sally Harding.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.120
So a literary agent
represents an author

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.250
and attempts to
market their books.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.310
And so they can be
extraordinarily useful people.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.830
And in return for that,
they get a percentage

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.009
of the book's profits and that's
how they make their living.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.630
So it's a difficult
job, especially now

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.010
because the market for
books is very complicated

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.610
and increasingly
restrictive, I would say,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.520
to a few extremely
successful authors.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.340
Anyways, Sally got a hold of me
and I'd thought for a long time

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.090
about writing a more popular
version of my first book called

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.860
Maps of Meaning.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.250
And I actually produced
almost a complete manuscript

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:43.740
that was let's call it a
simplified version or--

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.412
I wouldn't say simplified
exactly, more--

00:02:46.412 --> 00:02:48.120
well, I tried to make
it more accessible.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.520
But I was never
really happy with it.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.780
I never really felt that it
got its legs underneath it,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:55.754
I suppose.

00:02:55.754 --> 00:02:57.920
And maybe that's because I
was duplicating something

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.310
that I'd already done.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.070
I was thinking very hard
about what to do about this

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.680
and how I might approach it
because Sally was interested

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.290
and I thought it
might be a good thing

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:12.230
to try to write a book for
a more general audience.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:18.460
And we talked for a while
and we discussed a few ideas.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.770
This was back in 2012.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.010
And I mentioned to her that
I had written something

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:29.260
for Quora, which is
an online website,

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:32.500
obviously, where
anybody can post

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.450
any question and
anyone can answer it

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.210
and then the questions
are voted on.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:40.210
And Quota's quite
interesting, I would say.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.280
And I had written a
variety of answers

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.320
because I was playing
around with it.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.770
And some of them,
as it always is,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.620
attracted much more
attention than others.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.270
And somebody posted, I
think it was a young guy,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.980
posted this question, what
are the most valuable things

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.020
everyone should know?

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.720
And so I answered it.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.939
I listed, as it was,
42 rules to live by.

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:06.980
And some of them were kind
of amusing, I thought,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.380
and some of them
were dead serious.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.630
Anyways, it became very
popular on Quora and as of now,

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:18.310
it has 174,000
views and 2,500 up

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.930
votes, which makes it one of
Quota's most popular answers,

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.270
as it was soon
after I posted it.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.660
And I thought, well, people
really liked those rules,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.970
there was something about
them that appealed to them.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.700
So what if I write a
book based on the rules?

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.930
And so I'll read you the rules.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:36.520
OK.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.430
Maybe you'll find
them interesting.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.410
Tell the truth.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.250
Do not do things that
you hate, which I think

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.290
is a variant of
telling the truth

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.950
because you have to act out the
truth, as well as telling it.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.530
Act so that you can tell
the truth about how you act,

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.720
that's obviously related
to the first two.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.860
Pursue what is meaningful,
not what is expedient.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.840
If you have to
choose, be the one

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:09.940
who does things instead of the
one who is seen to do things.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:11.560
Pay attention.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.930
Assume that the person
you are listening to

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.714
might know something
you need to know.

00:05:17.714 --> 00:05:21.720
Listen to them hard enough so
that they will share with you.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.330
Plan and work diligently
to maintain the romance

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.680
in your relationships.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.450
Be careful who you
share good news with.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:34.260
Be careful who you
share bad news with.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.190
Make at least one thing better
every single place you go.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:41.140
Imagine who you
could be and then

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.870
aim single-mindedly at that.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:48.260
Do not allow yourself to
become arrogant or resentful.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.780
Try to make one room in your
house as beautiful as possible.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.330
Compare yourself to
who you were yesterday,

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.750
not to who someone
else is today.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:02.200
Work as hard as you possibly
can on at least one thing

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.730
and see what happens.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.950
If old memories
still make you cry,

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.860
write them down
carefully and completely.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.140
Maintain your
connections with people.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.010
Do not carelessly denigrate
social institutions

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.990
or artistic achievement.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.630
Treat yourself as
if you were someone

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.830
that you are
responsible for helping.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:28.550
Ask someone to do you a small
favor so that he or she can ask

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.910
you to do one in the future.

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:35.890
Make friends with people
who want the best for you.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.110
Do not try to rescue
someone who does not

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:41.740
want to be rescued and be very
careful about rescuing someone

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.410
who does.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:48.960
Nothing well done
is insignificant.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.090
Set your house in perfect order
before you criticize the world.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.950
Dress like the person
you want to be.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.460
Be precise in your speech.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:05.680
Stand up straight with
your shoulders back.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.240
Don't avoid
something frightening

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.520
if it stands in your
way, and don't do

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.907
unnecessarily dangerous things.

00:07:12.907 --> 00:07:15.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.620
Do not let your
children do anything

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.310
that makes you dislike them.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.130
Do not transform your
wife into a maid.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:33.799
Do not hide unwanted
things in the fog.

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:37.130
Notice that opportunity
lurks where responsibility

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:39.900
has been abdicated.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.630
Read something written
by someone great.

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:47.720
Pet a cat when you
encounter one on the street.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.320
Don't bother children when
they're skateboarding.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.590
Don't let bullies
get away with it.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:56.270
Write a letter to
the government if you

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:01.000
see something that needs
fixing and propose a solution.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.370
Remember that what
you do not yet know

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.550
is more important than
what you already know

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.160
and be grateful in
spite of your suffering.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:15.620
So those were the 40-odd rules,
and people were pretty happy

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:16.950
with them.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.480
And then something odd happened.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.860
So a friend of mine is a
novelist, Greg Hurwitz.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:26.030
And Greg wrote a book
that's become very popular

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.340
called Orphan X. And
one of the characters

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:32.270
in that book, the female lead I
would say, an off and on again

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.360
wrote romance interest
for the main character,

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.710
posted these rules on her
fridge and so they were

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:41.030
peppered throughout the book.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:44.520
So that was kind of an
interesting development,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.500
I thought.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.820
And then because they
were so popular and Quora,

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.830
I talked to Sally
about the possibility

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.370
of turning them into a book.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:56.980
And so my original vision
was to write 42 short essays.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.846
I thought that would be--

00:09:58.846 --> 00:10:00.660
and I was going to
call the book 42,

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.840
which I thought was quite
funny because if you remember,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.270
the answer to life, the
universe, and everything

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:09.410
from Douglas Adams was 42.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.810
A computer calculated that.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.320
So I thought it would be
amusing to write a book called

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.600
42 that contained
answers to life,

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.000
the universe, and everything.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.460
But when I started
writing the book,

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.360
it turned out that I had more
to say about each of the rules

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.530
than I had originally
envisioned.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:31.670
And so I first cut it down
to 25 and wrote some essays,

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.290
and then I cut it down to 16.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:36.110
And it was still
too long, and then I

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:37.730
cut it down to 12,
which turned out

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.190
to be a good thing anyways
because if you write a book,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.740
you should write way more
than you're going to publish

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:43.970
and then cut.

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:47.910
Because that way, you're
only left with what's best.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:53.114
So anyways, I wrote
for about three years--

00:10:53.114 --> 00:10:54.530
well, no, it's
more than that now.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:57.170
I guess it's almost five
years because I just

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:59.980
finished the book in September.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.120
So it took about four
or five years to write,

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.100
and I was writing
pretty regularly.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.400
What I'm going to do is read
to you a little bit of it,

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:08.480
I think.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.420
This is the book here.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.270
That's a galley copy,
and a galley copy

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:16.850
is a preprint of
a book so that you

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.520
can get some sense of
what it looks like.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.250
It's got some nice
illustrations in it, I think,

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:27.470
by this comic book author
named Ethan van Sciver.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.820
So that's kind of a rough
one on chapter three,

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.570
"Treat Yourself Like Someone You
are Responsible for Helping."

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:40.310
Ethan is a comic book artist,
he's a great Illustrator.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:44.780
And he put each one of my kids,
I have two kids, one of them

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:50.510
in each illustration
sort of contemplating

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.160
the complexities
of life, I guess

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:53.810
that's how you
might subscribe it.

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:55.007
So that's one illustration.

00:11:55.007 --> 00:11:56.090
I'll show you another one.

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:00.790
So that's my son,
Julian, there looking up

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:03.010
at the great statue of David.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:06.700
And David, of course, is
an inspirational statue.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.100
It's a representation
of what you could be.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.290
And that's rule
three, make friends

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.260
with people who want
the best for you.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:18.200
And I think I'm going to read
some of that to you right now.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.070
Each rule is divided
into subsections--

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:25.230
small subchapters, let's say--
and I'll read you one of those

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.520
so you'll get some
sense of the book.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:31.150
Rule three, make
friends with people

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:34.620
who want the best for you.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.130
The old hometown.

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:38.850
The town I grew up
in had been scraped

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:43.770
only 50 years earlier out of the
endless flat northern prairie.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.830
Fairview, Alberta was
part of the frontier

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.650
and had the cowboy
bars to prove it.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:53.250
The Hudson's Bay Company
department store on Main Street

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.740
still bought beaver,
wolf, and coyote furs

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.170
directly from the
local trappers.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:02.340
3,000 people lived there, 400
miles away from the nearest

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.060
city.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:09.700
Cable TV, video games, and
internet did not exist.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.130
It was no easy matter to
stay innocently amused

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:16.020
in Fairview, particularly
during the five months of winter

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:20.010
when long stretches of 40 below
days and even colder nights

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.350
were the norm.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:25.940
The world's a different place
when it's cold like that.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.580
The drunks in our town
ended their sad lives early.

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:32.490
They passed out in snow
banks at 3:00 in the morning,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.260
froze to death.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:38.180
You don't go outside
casually when it's 40 below.

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:43.630
On first breath, the arid desert
air constricts your lungs.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:47.470
Ice forms on your eyelashes
and they stick together.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:51.550
Long hair wet from the shower
freezes solid and then stands

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:56.020
on end, wraith-like of its own
accord later in a warm house

00:13:56.020 --> 00:14:00.460
when it thaws bone dry,
charged with electricity.

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:04.190
Children only put their tongues
on steel playground equipment

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:06.320
once.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.380
Smoke from house
chimneys doesn't rise.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:12.500
Defeated by the cold,
it drifts downwards

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:15.950
and collects like fog
on snow-covered rooftops

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:17.600
and yards.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.700
Cars must be
plugged in at night,

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:22.970
their engines warmed
by block heaters or oil

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:25.100
will not flow through
them in the morning

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:28.010
and they won't start.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.170
Sometimes they won't anyways.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.060
Then you turn the
engine over pointlessly

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.600
until the starter
clatters and falls silent.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.570
Then you remove the frozen
battery from the car,

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:42.200
loosening bolts with stiffening
figures in the intense cold,

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:44.650
and bring it into the house.

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:48.010
It sits there sweating
for hours until it warms

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:51.130
enough to hold a decent charge.

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:53.710
You're not going to see out of
the back window of your car,

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:54.515
either.

00:14:54.515 --> 00:14:58.420
It frosts over in November
and stays that way until May.

00:14:58.420 --> 00:15:02.020
Scraping it off just
dampens the upholstery,

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.210
then that freezes, too.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.490
Late one night going
to visit a friend,

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:10.580
I sat for two hours on the
edge of the passenger's seat

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:13.970
in a 1970 Dodge
Challenger jammed up

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:18.290
against the stick shift using
a vodka-soaked rag to keep

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:19.880
the inside of the
front windshield

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:23.360
clear in front of the driver
because the car heater had

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:24.410
quit.

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:26.930
Stopping wasn't an option.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:29.210
There was nowhere to stop.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.320
And it was hell on house cats.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.430
Felines in Fairview had
short ears and tails

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:38.110
because they had lost the
tips of both to frostbite.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.660
They came to resemble
arctic foxes, which

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:44.740
evolved those features
to deal proactively

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:46.990
with the intense cold.

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:49.780
One day our cat got
outside and no one noticed.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:52.600
We found him later,
fur frozen fast

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:57.250
to the cold, hard concrete,
with no lasting damage

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:59.080
except to his pride.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:03.580
Fairview cats were also at great
risk in the winter from cars,

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:06.340
but not for the
reasons you think.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.740
It wasn't automobiles
sliding on icy roads

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:09.820
and running them over.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:13.060
Only loser cats died that way.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:15.790
It was cars parked
immediately after being driven

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:17.230
that were dangerous.

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.960
A frigid cat might think
highly of climbing up

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:25.720
under such a vehicle and sitting
on its still warm engine block.

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.530
But what if the driver
decided to use the car again

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:32.680
before the engine cooled
down and the cat departed?

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:34.810
Let's just say that
heat-seeking house

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:38.080
pets and rapidly
rotating radiator fans

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.700
do not co-exist happily.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.270
Because we were so far north,
the bitterly cold winters

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.130
were very dark.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:51.120
By December, the sun
didn't rise until 10:00 AM.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.090
We trudged to school
in the pitch black.

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:56.100
It wasn't much lighter
when we walked home just

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.224
before the early sunset.

00:16:58.224 --> 00:16:59.640
There wasn't much
for young people

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.520
to do in Fairview,
even in the summer,

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.369
but the winters were worse.

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:09.730
Then your friends mattered
more than anything.

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:12.420
So that's a little section
of 12 Rules for Life.

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:14.609
I can tell you
what the rules are.

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:17.190
It's foreword was
written by Norman Doidge.

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:20.617
He comments a little bit
about the political turmoil

00:17:20.617 --> 00:17:22.200
that surrounded me
over the last year,

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:26.430
although the book itself is very
apolitical with the exception

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:29.790
of the chapter on
skateboarding, where

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:34.620
I go into the ongoing,
polarizing battle between I

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:37.350
would say the radical
left and everyone else.

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:41.700
But other than that, it's
not a political book.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.700
So the table of
contents includes

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:48.360
rule one, stand up straight
with your shoulders back.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:51.000
That's actually a
story that centers

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.640
on lobsters to a large degree.

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:58.620
I have a proclivity for
talking about lobsters.

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:01.780
Rule two, treat yourself
like someone you

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:03.444
are responsible for helping.

00:18:03.444 --> 00:18:04.860
That's something
that people don't

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:07.920
do very well because they're
not very happy with themselves

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:09.240
often.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.880
Rule three, make
friends with people

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.440
who want the best for you.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.840
That's the chapter
I already read.

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.010
I read the first part of it.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:21.630
And it's about trying
to organize your life

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.250
so that you're
surrounded by people

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:27.360
who support you when you're
trying to do what's right.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.720
It's very important.

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:32.870
Rule four, compare yourself
to who you were yesterday

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:36.650
and not to who
someone else is today.

00:18:36.650 --> 00:18:39.500
Because if you're comparing
yourself to someone else,

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:41.480
I mean, first of all,
you don't know very much

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:44.540
about the life of the person
you are comparing yourself to;

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:48.020
you don't know it across
all of its dimensions.

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:50.010
And second, people
are very different

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:52.860
and so comparing
yourself to someone else

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:55.130
is kind of useful, I
guess, when you're young.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:58.010
But as you get older and more
singular and more particular,

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:01.670
it becomes increasingly
less useful.

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:04.700
Better to compare yourself to
a previous version of yourself

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:08.150
and work for
improvement in that way.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:11.284
Rule five, do not
let your children

00:19:11.284 --> 00:19:12.950
do anything that makes
you dislike them.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.870
That's probably going to be
the most controversial chapter,

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:16.370
I would say.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:23.310
It's a chapter about discipline
and love and respect.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.050
I guess that's probably right.

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:28.690
Rule six, set your
house in perfect order

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.591
before you criticize the world.

00:19:30.591 --> 00:19:31.090
Yeah.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:35.160
Well, that's a
rough chapter, man.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:37.000
I'm trying to
explain the mindset

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:40.610
behind people like the
Columbine High School shooters.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:44.770
And so it's a harsh chapter.

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:47.320
Rule seven, pursue
what is meaningful

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.840
and not what is expedient.

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.620
Expedient is what works
in the short term,

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:54.850
expedient is what's easy.

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:58.900
Meaningful, instead, is what
sustains you through tragedy,

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:00.480
let's say.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:05.020
Rule eight, tell the truth,
or at least don't lie.

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:05.520
Yeah.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.920
Well, it's not that easy to
tell the truth because who

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:09.060
knows about the truth?

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:11.700
But you can learn
not to say things

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:13.090
that you know to be false.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:16.260
And if you stop saying things
that you know to be false,

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:18.090
then your life
will improve a lot.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:22.080
It simplifies it and it puts
you in alignment with reality.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.300
And you should be in
alignment with reality

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:27.840
because there's a lot more
of it than there is of you.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.870
Rule nine, assume that the
person you're listening

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.690
to might know
something you don't.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:36.990
That's a good one because--
and this is even true when

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:40.710
you're dealing with people that
don't like you because they may

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:43.560
offer you criticisms
that are valid--

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.200
many that aren't, too, of course
because maybe they're just

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:47.940
trying to take you down a bit.

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:50.550
But if you get a
criticism that's valid

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:54.360
and then you can fix
that error, let's say,

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.270
that'll stop you from running
nose first into walls,

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.490
and that's generally
a handy thing.

00:20:59.490 --> 00:21:03.221
Rule 10, be precise
in your speech.

00:21:03.221 --> 00:21:03.720
Yeah.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.540
Well, you have to be
exacting in the way

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:08.520
that you subscribe
things and the way

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:10.890
that you think because
otherwise, you live in the fog

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:14.010
and you won't get what
you should out of life

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:16.420
or what you need out of life.

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:19.530
And by need, I mean what will
sustain you so that you don't

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:22.720
become bitter and resentful.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:27.130
Rule 11, do not bother children
when they're skateboarding.

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:32.530
So that chapter is about I would
say our society's hyper concern

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:35.530
with safety and its
fundamental antagonism

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:40.140
to exploratory behavior,
especially among boys.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:44.290
Rule 12, pet a cat when you
encounter one on the street.

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:44.790
Yeah.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:51.390
Well, that's about trying to
orient yourself towards life so

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:54.120
that even when you're in
the middle of a crisis

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:59.830
or a tragedy, you can find bits
and pieces of experience that

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:06.340
are positive and sustaining so
that you can stay positively

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:09.520
oriented towards your own
existence and towards existence

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:11.300
itself.

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:16.010
So anyways, that's the book.

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:19.880
And it's coming
out January 23rd,

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:22.670
although it's
available for preorder

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:27.200
at Amazon.com or Amazon.ca
and several other places.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:31.280
I'll put links in the
video description.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:34.790
If you're trying to figure
out how to orient yourself

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:37.970
in the midst of the chaos
that constitutes life,

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:42.050
then you might find some of the
things that I wrote in 12 Rules

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:43.490
for Life Helpful.

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:48.410
So I hope that that was
interesting and goodbye

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:50.140
for now.

